        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> Half of new code is taken from other `mini.nvim` modules (`H.user_input` and `H.echo` functions). I could use regular `vim.fn.input` and `print` but thought that using already written function is more stable approach. The rest of new code is like: get user input, check it and either remove or modify it. IMHO, it does not look like overkill functionality.\r\n\r\nAround 60 new lines are added just for this functionality, which is already too much for the 'mini.pick' at its current state. And occupying two mappings is also a bit too much for my taste. The better initial approach might have been for `<C-o>` to be followed by an \"action identifier\" (like `a` for \"add\", `e` for \"edit\", `d` for \"delete\"). But it is too big of a change now and adds to many layers to my liking.\r\n\r\nI am sorry, but currently I don't think this addition to 'mini.pick' follows the overall design of 'mini.nvim' (prioritize maintenance-features balance instead of adding more features).",
